# Tau-scoped AppArmor profile — Spike W2, Linux stock-VM netns lane.
#
# PURPOSE
# -------
# Ubuntu 24.04 (and Debian 13) ship kernel.apparmor_restrict_unprivileged_userns=1. Under it the
# kernel still permits unshare(CLONE_NEWUSER) for an unprivileged process, but STRIPS the
# capabilities from the resulting user namespace UNLESS the executing binary has an AppArmor profile
# that carries the `userns,` permission. bwrap's loopback bring-up (RTM_NEWADDR to add 127.0.0.1/8
# to `lo` inside its fresh --unshare-net netns) needs CAP_NET_ADMIN in that namespace; with the caps
# stripped it fails with `bwrap: loopback: Failed RTM_NEWADDR: Operation not permitted`, and the
# sandbox never launches. @anthropic-ai/sandbox-runtime 0.0.75 always issues --unshare-net and brings
# loopback up even for a deny-all-egress profile, so this blocks EVERY sandboxed shell, not only
# networked ones.
#
# Stock Ubuntu ships exactly this shape for the DISTRO bwrap at /usr/bin/bwrap
# (/etc/apparmor.d/bwrap-userns-restrict: `profile bwrap /usr/bin/bwrap flags=(unconfined) { userns, }`),
# which is why `apt install bubblewrap` + /usr/bin/bwrap keeps working. Tau does NOT run the distro
# bwrap: an Electron desktop app ships its OWN bwrap bundled under its install prefix (/opt/<App>/…),
# and the stock profile's attachment path (/usr/bin/bwrap) does not match that path, so Tau's bundled
# bwrap falls under the restriction and loopback setup fails.
#
# This profile re-establishes the grant for TAU'S bundled bwrap path ONLY. It is the Electron-app
# packaging pattern (Google Chrome ships /etc/apparmor.d/chrome attaching to /opt/google/chrome/chrome
# for the identical reason). It is installed by `apparmor_parser` at .deb install time (postinst), NOT
# by running the sandboxed workload elevated.
#
# WHAT IT IS NOT
# --------------
#   * It does NOT touch kernel.apparmor_restrict_unprivileged_userns — that stays at its stock value 1.
#   * It does NOT weaken the system-wide default or any other binary. Attachment is a single absolute
#     path; nothing else on the system changes behaviour.
#   * `flags=(unconfined)` scopes the permissiveness to processes exec'd from THIS path. It mirrors the
#     distro bwrap profile exactly; the safety comes from the narrow attachment, not from internal
#     confinement. (A fully enumerated confined profile is possible but would have to re-derive bwrap's
#     entire mount/cap/bind surface; the distro itself chose the unconfined+userns form.)
#
# WHY ONE PROFILE COVERS apply-seccomp TOO
# ----------------------------------------
# SRT's seccomp helper (apply-seccomp) creates its OWN nested user namespace to obtain CAP_SYS_ADMIN
# for its PID+mount unshare. It is exec'd as a descendant INSIDE bwrap's namespace, so it runs under
# this profile's label; `flags=(unconfined)` + `userns,` grants the nested userns the same way Chrome's
# single profile covers its zygote/namespace children. No second profile is required. (If a future
# packaging runs apply-seccomp as a standalone, pre-bwrap step from a distinct path, add a second
# `profile tau-apply-seccomp /opt/Tau/resources/sandbox/apply-seccomp flags=(unconfined) { userns, }`.)
#
# ATTACHMENT PATH
# ---------------
# /opt/Tau/resources/sandbox/bwrap is the representative bundled path for a .deb that installs under
# /opt/Tau (electron-builder's default target dir is /opt/${productName}). The lane's run-in-guest.sh
# stages the guest's own /usr/bin/bwrap at this path (same bytes, different path → stock profile does
# not attach) to reproduce Tau's shipping layout faithfully. Change this single path to match the real
# productName/prefix chosen for the Tau .deb; keep it in sync with the postinst that calls
# apparmor_parser and with the sandbox-runtime `bwrapPath` Tau passes.

abi <abi/4.0>,

include <tunables/global>

profile tau-bwrap /opt/Tau/resources/sandbox/bwrap flags=(unconfined) {
  userns,

  # Site-local overrides (empty by default); present so an operator can tighten without editing the
  # packaged profile, mirroring the distro bwrap profile's `include if exists <local/bwrap>`.
  include if exists <local/tau-bwrap>
}
